    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's mostly just the ttl and the different ISPs caching the old data? i'm just trying to understand if there's anything actually happening on the network side or if it's just waiting for timers to expire.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's correct. The Time to Live (TTL) tells DNS servers how long to cache the A record, while ISPs and other DNS resolvers cache the information for as long as the TTL specifies. It's not just a network issue, but also involves DNS resolvers acting on the TTL instead of querying the authoritative server for the updated information every time. Once the TTL expires and they do query again, they'll receive the new IP address, assuming it has been updated at the authoritative DNS server.",
      "source": "target",
